  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Every morning thousands of floating laborers assemble at open-air hiring points across Dhaka and offer the only asset most of them possess, a working body. This article asks how subsistence and social status are produced in these markets and argues that they are made together through a public process of bodily valuation. A mixed-methods design combined an intercept survey of 749 day laborers with ethnographic fieldwork at seven hiring sites, including forty-two days of direct observation, between May 2025 and February 2026. Workers attended a market 6.2 days a week yet worked only 4.1 of the previous 7 days, earned a median of BDT 2,800 a week, and 74.0% were moderately or severely food insecure. Three in five had been injured at work in the past year, and discrimination at hiring was reported chiefly on grounds of physical capacity and age. A work injury carried the largest adjusted association with food insecurity, nearly three times the odds, while skilled trade raised the daily wage without protecting the household, and women earned about 15% less than men and reported bodily and age discrimination more often. Ethnographically, workers manage waiting, conceal illness, and sustain one another through tea-stall credit and job information, brokers both open access and take cuts, and the state, present through identity cards and mobile money, is absent as a labor institution. The hiring site is theorized as a bodily valuation regime, and city-level policies to make these markets reliable, fairly priced, and answerable are identified.",
