  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on non-probability intercept sampling across seven specific hiring sites, which may not capture all segments of Dhaka's broader informal labor force.",
      "what": "Using an intercept survey of 749 laborers alongside 42 days of observation across seven hiring points in Dhaka between May 2025 and February 2026, this study examines open-air day labor dynamics. Workers attended hiring points 6.2 days per week but secured work on only 4.1 days, earning a median weekly wage of BDT 2,800 with 74.0% experiencing moderate to severe food insecurity. Prior-year workplace injuries affected 60% of laborers and nearly tripled the odds of household food insecurity, while female workers faced a 15% wage discount and higher rates of age and physical capacity discrimination.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that informal labor markets operate through a public regime of physical valuation where physical injury is the primary driver of household crisis, proving that informal skill accumulation fails to shield vulnerable urban workers from severe food poverty."
    }
  },
